  {headings}
>

<h2 id="build">Build the binary</h2>

You need Go 1.27, Node 24, pnpm 11, and [just](https://github.com/casey/just). Node is a build-time dependency only; the console is embedded in the binary.

```sh
git clone https://github.com/tylergannon/gimbal
cd gimbal
just build
```

`just build` generates the Go bindings, builds the skgo/SvelteKit app, checks
for `web/build/skgo.manifest.json`, and packages the app into `web/build.zip`
for embedding in `bin/gimbal`. To install from this checkout, run
`go install ./cmd/gimbal` after `just build`. Starting with v0.12.1, you can
also install a release directly with
`go install github.com/tylergannon/gimbal/cmd/gimbal@<version>`; its packaged
web app starts without a frontend build on the installing machine.

Gimbal drives coding-agent harnesses you already have. Install and sign in to at least one of Codex, Claude Code, or Antigravity before running a workflow.

<h2 id="run">Run a built-in workflow</h2>

`review` is the smallest built-in workflow: one reviewer, read-only, structured findings. Point it at any repository.

```sh
./bin/gimbal run review \
  --work-dir ~/src/your-project \
  --goal "Find correctness bugs in the payment retry logic"
```

Every role has a default model, shown in `--help`. Override one for a single run with its flag, as `model` or `model:effort`:

```sh
./bin/gimbal run review --goal "..." --code-review flash
```

<h2 id="watch">Watch it</h2>

The run serves the console on [http://127.0.0.1:8080](http://127.0.0.1:8080) while it works. Afterward, `gimbal` with no subcommand serves every run recorded under the project's `.gimbal` directory.

<figure>
  <Shot src="shots/runs.png" alt="The runs list with status, latest activity, cost, elapsed time, sessions, and turns for each run." />
</figure>

From a terminal, `gimbal runs` lists runs in progress, `gimbal watch RUN` follows one, and `gimbal steer RUN MESSAGE --session ID` sends a message into a running agent turn.

<h2 id="library">Use the library</h2>

The runtime is an ordinary Go package. Its contract is its Godoc and compiling examples.

```sh
go get github.com/tylergannon/gimbal
go doc -all github.com/tylergannon/gimbal
```

A complete program, from the package examples:

```go
err := gimbal.Run(ctx, "example", models, func(ctx context.Context) error {
	gimbal.Set(ctx, "goal", "demonstrate the public API")
	worker := gimbal.NewSession(ctx, "worker", ".")
	answer, err := worker.Generate[gimbal.Text](ctx, "Complete the goal.")
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	fmt.Println(answer)
	return nil
})
```

`models` maps each role the workflow names to a harness adapter, a model, and an effort. The workflow never names a model.

</DocsPage>
